As I understand, each armor/weapon is vulnerable to at most one of
fire/rust.  In general, metal objects are fireproof to start with, and
cloth objects are rustproof by nature.  (Some are safe from
both---like the shield of reflection.)

Thus, there is only one bit (computer jargon, don't worry if you don't
know that this means) in each armor that says if it is erodeproof or
not.  When the armor/weapon gets erodeproofed ("your *** is covered by
a shimmering golden shield!"), that bit is set, and it will be
resistant to both attacks.

This leads to an interesting question---if you polymorph an armor,
will its erodeproofness stay put even if its vulnerabilyty changes?
For instance, if a fireproof speed boots gets polymorphed into a pair
of gauntlets of power, will they become rustproof?  (I know the
blessed status, damage and enchantment stays the same, so my guess to
the above question is "yes".)

By the way, there is another attack called "rot" (from brown puddings
and such) which I believe affects both types...but I'm not too sure
about this.  Maybe it was "rot" for non-fireproof objects and "rust"
for non-rustproof objects....

